#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120601} :activity/id #uuid "291c939d-6c10-57d8-bddb-ee6fe28fa722" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048151 :topic/id #uuid "838443f8-2681-52c2-95ba-8d93abeaf009" :topic/math-academy-id 1839} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120601 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121011 :tutorial/content "For a plane curve $C$ defined by the vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle x(t), \\: y(t) \\rangle,\n$$\n\nthe *curvature* of $C$ at an arbitrary point can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t)  = \\dfrac{|x'(t)y''(t) - y'(t)x''(t)|}{ (x'(t)^2 + y'(t)^2)^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, the **signed curvature** $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t) = \\dfrac{x'(t)y''(t) - y'(t)x''(t)}{ (x'(t)^2 + y'(t)^2)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider a curve defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx(t) = 2t, \\qquad\ny(t) = 1+t^2.\n$$\n\nLet's find the curvature of this curve at the point where $t=1.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the first and second derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{′}(t)=2,\\, & {y}^{′}(t)=2t \\\\  & {x}^{″}(t)=0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these expressions into our curvature formula, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2\\cdot 2-2t\\cdot 0|}{{[{2}^{2}+(2t)^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,4\\,|}{{2}^{3}{[1+{t}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{({t}^{2}+1)}^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t =1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =\\frac{1}{2{({1}^{2}+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b40dd6c7-fe0a-5126-9014-ec7c0d5eb29b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2534 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "402c2124-8063-56c7-bb37-ea2cdcc0a66c" :step/math-academy-id 11439 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120606}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120602 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119761 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119577 :question/id #uuid "1f374865-c7f7-510c-b361-fbad352d86c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-3452"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36986a18-172c-5983-8f6f-3211b6e8f89d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048339}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481419}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"} :step/id #uuid "71cffa1e-47f4-55d9-a319-71cf51f73286" :step/math-academy-id 11440 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120604}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120603 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121012 :tutorial/content "We've been using the following formula for unsigned curvature:\n\n$$\n\\kappa (t)  = \\dfrac{|x'(t)y''(t) - y'(t)x''(t)|}{ (x'(t)^2 + y'(t)^2)^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nTo see where this formula comes from, remember that for a curve $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle x(t),  y(t),  z(t)\\rangle,$ the unsigned curvature formula is\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t)\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}.\n$$\n\nIf a curve does not have a $z$-component, then we may consider $z(t) = 0.$ Thus, any plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle x(t), \\: y(t) \\rangle$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle x(t), \\:  y(t), \\: 0\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\mathbf r''(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t) = \\langle x'(t), y'(t),0 \\rangle, \\qquad \\mathbf r''(t) = \\langle x''(t), y''(y),0 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSubstituting these expressions into our cross-product formula, we find that the curvature $\\kappa(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{‖⟨{x}^{′}(t),{y}^{′}(t),0⟩\\times ⟨{x}^{″}(t),{y}^{″}(t),0⟩‖}{‖⟨{x}^{′}(t),{y}^{′}(t)⟩{‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{‖⟨0,0,{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)⟩‖}{(\\sqrt{{x}^{′}(t)^{2}+{y}^{′}(t)^{2}})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{({x}^{′}(t)^{2}+{y}^{′}(t)^{2})^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "16d4e0f1-81e9-5252-9390-52f2e8512d48" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2535 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formula for the Curvature of a Plane Parametric Curve"} :step/id #uuid "f6ca150c-baf4-5085-bb7d-62a05a15bc30" :step/math-academy-id 11441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120604 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121013 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a plane curve defined by the explicit equation \n\n$$\ny = y(x).\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can parametrize this curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle t, \\: y(t) \\rangle.\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $x=t,$ we calculate the first and second derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′}(x) \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & ={y}^{″}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the (unsigned) curvature, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|1\\cdot {y}^{″}(x)-{y}^{′}(x)\\cdot 0|}{({1}^{2}+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unsigned curvature for $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa (x) = \\dfrac{|y''(x)|}{ (1 + [y'(x)]^2)^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nand the signed curvature is\n\n$$\nk(x) = \\dfrac {y''(x)} {(1+[y'(x)]^2)^{3/2}}\t.\n$$\n\nNote that the signed curvature $k(x)$ has the same sign as $y''(x).$ Therefore,\n\n- if $k(x) > 0,$ the curve is concave up, and\n\n- if $k(x) < 0,$ the curve is concave down." :tutorial/id #uuid "66d1756a-304e-51f3-9622-3074a6e63c70" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2536 :tutorial/title "The Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Cartesian Form"} :step/id #uuid "e2fbbaa6-20ed-599e-ba35-826ad87a33df" :step/math-academy-id 11444 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120605}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120605 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119762 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119578 :question/id #uuid "2ff129e7-a923-5510-a35b-a6be403152ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-3453"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "384072a8-f008-5279-bb58-8086eb42b710" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444928}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Cartesian Form"} :step/id #uuid "a177d231-4807-586b-8e78-fdd08a9a7171" :step/math-academy-id 11445 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120603}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119763 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119579 :question/id #uuid "d8e470ad-6a9a-5a03-a90e-b21b24cb6862" :question/math-academy-id "e-3454"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "814655d8-f459-5df5-b722-3024b87065db" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048648}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444932}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"} :step/id #uuid "8c7bc743-0c19-5cb7-bd3d-35d253cf54f2" :step/math-academy-id 11449 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120602}}] :activity/title "The Curvature of a Plane Curve" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120038}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481457} :activity/id #uuid "b23cf47b-075d-5a97-9891-e94b2303d1ed" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048151 :topic/id #uuid "838443f8-2681-52c2-95ba-8d93abeaf009" :topic/math-academy-id 1839} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481452 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0990170c-4d1e-50a2-98e8-151e0ba11892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{e}{({e}^{2}-e+2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278684} {:answer/id #uuid "4d60cb8a-dc24-56ef-af99-b175cf20b062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{e}{({e}^{2}-2e+2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279289} {:answer/id #uuid "d83ba20e-641d-5922-b928-055d7e54093d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{({e}^{2}-e+2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280502} {:answer/id #uuid "e3b8ebcb-d501-5c6a-8146-bd7048b4d0df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{({e}^{2}-2e+2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280602} {:answer/id #uuid "e74cacf6-957c-5ea2-816a-02197cc75801" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{e}{({e}^{2}-e+1)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280630}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279289} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0741ee1-638a-5f79-a6d9-00b3eb6de84c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7fbc67aa-6887-5c1b-ba6e-44712c2e9cc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162535" :question/problem "Find the signed curvature of the curve $y=x-{e}^{x}$ at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(x)=\\frac{{y}^{″}(x)}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=1-{e}^{x},\\,{y}^{″}(x)=-{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(x) & =\\frac{-{e}^{x}}{{[1+{(1-{e}^{x})}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{(1+1+{e}^{2x}-2{e}^{x})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{(2+{e}^{2x}-2{e}^{x})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(1) & =-\\frac{{e}^{(1)}}{{(2+{e}^{2(1)}-2{e}^{(1)})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{e}{({e}^{2}-2e+2)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5984cdf7-b8da-5f68-9d43-060824a70ec7"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481457 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67eae735-49cf-5cfd-885a-9dcf6358fe9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319710} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed4b8f4-ef8b-53cb-b447-80eb51ba9fa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319772} {:answer/id #uuid "898f8cb2-0234-5a1e-b3da-6232517cf135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319801} {:answer/id #uuid "af23b294-572a-5369-b57c-4a4d14558963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319924} {:answer/id #uuid "bd4e6d3d-d544-5c9e-bc7e-3b613641fec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319968}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319772} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1eba548-5a1d-560c-8002-dd475d44f6ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5c3279eb-5aad-5467-9349-e9a454f6c445" :question/math-academy-id "q-35190" :question/problem "Find the signed curvature of the curve $y=2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+1$ at $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(x)=\\frac{{y}^{″}(x)}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=6{x}^{2}-2x,\\,{y}^{″}(x)=12x-2\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(x) & =\\frac{12x-2}{{[1+{(6{x}^{2}-2x)}^{2}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(0) & =\\frac{12(0)-2}{{[1+{(6\\cdot {0}^{2}-2\\cdot 0)}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{{1}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7989c10f-301e-5fd5-a99d-843967d148ab" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481466}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481458 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e0f5b62-beae-5da9-9733-3f30198a3fe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480913} {:answer/id #uuid "a9439103-3642-5537-a910-2dc11e3a90cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481059} {:answer/id #uuid "aac9c95c-bf17-5a76-803e-802d8b34fb5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481060} {:answer/id #uuid "ef4f4a60-5e88-55c6-a337-0cc6ded7df2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481197} {:answer/id #uuid "f37bf78f-c249-5ffb-8bbc-e56ecf9ea0dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481212}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481059} :answer-field/id #uuid "21292018-ea56-5ae6-a7f5-dac08271bdff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cf02b7af-f8d0-5528-8cc7-2d359506a141" :question/math-academy-id "q-162426" :question/problem "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)=(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,i+t\\,j$ at $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=(1+{t}^{2})^{2},\\,y(t)=t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =4t(1+{t}^{2}),\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =12{t}^{2}+4,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|4t(1+{t}^{2})\\cdot 0-1\\cdot (12{t}^{2}+4)|}{{((4t(1+{t}^{2}))^{2}+{1}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12{t}^{2}+4}{{((4t(1+{t}^{2}))^{2}+1)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{12(0)^{2}+4}{{((4(0)(1+(0)^{2}))^{2}+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{(0+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7d616550-73e3-5af7-9de1-ca6f69c3484f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481452}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481464 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281473 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41d1b705-7f9e-5fc0-b8a3-f2ec630dec22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279177} {:answer/id #uuid "62dc1fd8-4359-5dfe-8e43-f5d26808e6c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279489} {:answer/id #uuid "b079896f-a2ab-52d8-960e-3fcea974815a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280156} {:answer/id #uuid "b772997e-b578-5476-85da-375092d1f621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280203} {:answer/id #uuid "e2cd2225-4e85-578e-9f9b-33c02aa80b66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280595}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280203} :answer-field/id #uuid "d136c6b3-3d9c-5690-a6fb-a9cb6fc2517a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c9434c2d-4cb6-586b-bed8-8562887abbb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-162424" :question/problem "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)=2{e}^{t}\\,i+{e}^{2t}\\,j$ at $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=2{e}^{t},\\,y(t)={e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2{e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =2{e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =4{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2{e}^{t}\\cdot 4{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{2t}\\cdot 2{e}^{t}|}{{((2{e}^{t})^{2}+(2{e}^{2t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{3t}}{{(4{e}^{2t}+4{e}^{4t})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{(1+{e}^{2t})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{1}{2{(1+{e}^{2(0)})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{(1+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b2354326-e32e-55ec-8498-3dfd11986f23" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481458}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481466 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481345 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aebd026-ca42-5f7b-a004-54acb6bb5bca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480794} {:answer/id #uuid "49588260-15d3-5737-a45c-19014de13a38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480877} {:answer/id #uuid "765aabea-15c0-5261-b55c-6580766856e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480955} {:answer/id #uuid "be7a929c-eeb7-5ef6-835a-ffe7851d6867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481102} {:answer/id #uuid "e7526dc4-7140-5b54-9556-75659724e253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481181}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480877} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5755c53-01de-5e40-9f46-4f16d8b88aa1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "08715ccd-f649-5c84-af70-2145bc128aa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-162396" :question/problem "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+({t}^{2}-t)\\,j$ at $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{2},\\,y(t)={t}^{2}-t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2t,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =2t-1 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =2,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2t\\cdot 2-(2t-1)\\cdot 2|}{{((2t)^{2}+(2t-1)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(8{t}^{2}-4t+1)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{2}{{(8(0)^{2}-4(0)+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(0-0+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c27dc5ec-712e-5a98-ada6-dc30058e6fc4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481464}}] :activity/title "The Curvature of a Plane Curve" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480734}]}
